  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a cross-sectional design, which captures spatial correlation at a single point in time rather than the causal dynamics of supermarket placement or changing neighborhood demographics.",
      "what": "This study analyzed spatial access to discount food supermarkets across England using Geographic Information Systems (GIS) and quantile regression. The model controlled for population density, conventional supermarkets, urbanization levels, public transport availability, and north-south regional differences to measure how deprivation relates to physical proximity. The analysis revealed that more deprived areas generally have higher physical accessibility to discount food supermarkets, though this relationship varies significantly between urban and rural areas as well as between the north and south of the country.",
      "why": "It establishes that discount supermarkets are physically closer to deprived populations who need them most, shifting the policy focus from simple physical availability to how regional and urban-rural disparities affect food security strategies."
    }
